The emulated A2065 hands the guest back its own multicast frames, and IPv6
never gets an address.

WinUAE's receive filter already drops a frame the card itself sent when the
destination is a unicast address ("not for me2") or the broadcast address
("not for me3"). Multicast is not covered, so on a bridged run every frame the
guest sends to a multicast destination that the host link reflects arrives
back at the card and is delivered.

For IPv4 that is invisible: a reflected mDNS query is answered by nobody and
dropped. For IPv6 it is fatal. Duplicate address detection works by sending a
neighbour solicitation for the tentative address to its solicited-node
multicast group, and a solicitation for that address arriving from elsewhere
is exactly what a duplicate looks like. The guest hears its own, withdraws the
address, and does that for the link-local one as well -- so it configures no
IPv6 address at all, has no source to send from, and every command answers
"there is no route to that network". Measured on winbuilder 2026-07-28: five
multicast frames sent, five received back, none of the broadcast or unicast
ones. The stack cannot tell the difference, because the solicitation is
correct in every respect and RFC 4861 forbids the source link-layer address
option on it -- the only evidence is the ethernet source address, which the IP
layer never sees.

Real hardware on a real switch does not do this, and neither does SLIRP, which
is why nothing found it before a bridged run.

Apply to a WinUAE checkout (C:\winuae-src on winbuilder) before building; the
build recipe is in docs/RESEARCH.md 63.5.

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

--- a/a2065.cpp
+++ b/a2065.cpp
@@ -384,6 +384,17 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	// dst = multicast and src = me? our own frame, reflected back by the
+	// host link. The two tests above catch that for unicast and broadcast
+	// and nothing catches it here, which breaks IPv6 duplicate address
+	// detection: the guest reads its own solicitation as another node
+	// claiming the address.
+	if ((dstmac[0] & 0x01) && memcmp (srcmac, realmac, sizeof realmac) == 0) {
+		if (log_a2065 > 1)
+			write_log (_T("not for me4\n"));
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (log_a2065 > 1) {
 		if (!memcmp(dstmac, realmac, sizeof realmac)) {
 			write_log(_T("DST = ME. SRC = %02X.%02X.%02X.%02X.%02X.%02X\n"), srcmac[6], srcmac[7], srcmac[8], srcmac[9], srcmac[10], srcmac[11]);
